---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const steps = [
  { n: '01', t: 'Discovery',  d: 'One working session. Your customers, your competition, and how work actually reaches you today.', you: '60–90 min', me: 'Research and notes' },
  { n: '02', t: 'Strategy',   d: 'Page structure, conversion path and search targets, written down and agreed before any design.', you: 'Review', me: 'Structure document' },
  { n: '03', t: 'Content',    d: 'I write it, you correct it. Nobody hands you a spreadsheet and asks you to fill in your own website.', you: '~1 hour', me: 'All copy' },
  { n: '04', t: 'Design',     d: 'Every page type, desktop and mobile, at full fidelity before production code exists.', you: 'One round of notes', me: 'Design' },
  { n: '05', t: 'Build',      d: 'Fast, accessible, semantic. The performance budget is enforced by the build, not checked at the end.', you: 'Nothing', me: 'Build and QA' },
  { n: '06', t: 'Review',     d: 'You see it on a real URL and mark it up. Revisions run to the number named in your package.', you: '1–2 hours', me: 'Revisions' },
  { n: '07', t: 'Testing',    d: 'Keyboard, screen reader, real devices, forms end to end. Every link and every state.', you: 'Nothing', me: 'Full test pass' },
  { n: '08', t: 'Launch',     d: 'Your domain connected, redirects mapped, analytics verified, Search Console connected. I handle the technical setup and the switch.', you: 'Approve go-live', me: 'Launch' },
  { n: '09', t: 'Your choice', d: 'A defined post-launch window, then you decide: I keep looking after the site, or the project is handed over to you. Not a silence, and not a subscription you are stuck in.', you: 'Decide', me: 'Care or handover' },
];
---
<Base
  title="Process | JP Silva Digital"
  description="Nine steps from discovery to launch, with your total time commitment stated up front — around four hours."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="mark"><b>§01</b> Process</p>
      <div class="head">
        <h1 class="display">Nine steps. About four hours of your time.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          The most common reason a website project drags on for months is that the client
          quietly gets handed the work. Here is exactly who does what.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="rows">
        {steps.map((s) => (
          <li class="row prow">
            <span class="row__n">{s.n}</span>
            <div class="row__b">
              <p class="h3">{s.t}</p>
              <p class="small">{s.d}</p>
            </div>
            <span class="prow__m">
              <span class="prow__l">You</span>{s.you}
              <span class="prow__l prow__l--2">Me</span>{s.me}
            </span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <div class="note pnote">
        <span class="lbl">If you already have a website</span>
        <p class="small">
          Send me the link before step one. I review what exists &mdash; pages, content, branding,
          domain setup, the SEO foundation that is visible from outside &mdash; and tell you whether
          it is worth improving what you have or building a better custom version. For a rebuild
          your current site stays live the whole time, useful content and important page addresses
          carry forward, and your existing domain connects to the new site once you approve it.
          You do not need a new domain.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="note pnote">
        <span class="lbl">After launch: care, or handover</span>
        <p class="small">
          <strong>Website Care</strong> is for businesses that would rather not think about it:
          hosting and deployment, keeping the site running, backups, monitoring and technical
          support within an agreed scope. <strong>Website Care + Content Updates</strong> adds a
          set number of small content changes each month &mdash; a photo swapped, a paragraph
          changed, hours or a phone number updated, an existing service description edited, a
          button or link changed. New pages, new functionality, new integrations and full copy
          rewrites are project work and are scoped separately. There is no unlimited tier, because
          an honest limit is worth more than a promise neither of us can hold.
        </p>
        <p class="small">
          Prefer to run it yourself? The finished project is handed over with the access and
          documentation you need. Either way the website you paid for is yours.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="note pnote">
        <span class="lbl">What I guarantee, and what I don't</span>
        <p class="small">
          Scope, price and launch date — in writing, before work starts, with a stated remedy
          if I miss the date for reasons on my side. Not rankings, not lead volume, not
          revenue. Nobody can promise those honestly, and the ones who do are selling
          something else.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="btn-row pcta">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/free-demo">Start step one</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="mailto:jpsilva0418@gmail.com">Ask me a question</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .prow { grid-template-columns: 3.5rem 1fr auto; align-items: start; }
  .prow__m { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .8125rem; color: var(--graphite);
             text-align: right; padding-top: .25rem; display: grid; gap: .125rem; }
  .prow__l { display: block; font-size: var(--fs-label); letter-spacing: .1em;
             text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--rule-hi); }
  .prow__l--2 { margin-top: var(--s2); }
  @media (max-width: 52rem) {
    .prow { grid-template-columns: 2.5rem 1fr; }
    .prow__m { grid-column: 2; text-align: left; padding-top: var(--s3); }
  }
  .pnote { margin-top: var(--s6); max-width: 52rem; }
  .pcta { margin-top: var(--s6); }
</style>
